# ffmpeg — resize / downscale an image (aspect, even-dims, quality)

0.1.0 Use when you have a still image and need to scale it — downscale to a max dimension (e.g. vision ≤1568), fit a box without distortion, or resize for delivery. Covers the -2/-1 even-dimension rule, no-upscale guards, and lanczos vs bilinear quality.

You have a still image (PNG/JPG/etc.) and need to change its pixel dimensions: shrink a 4000px render so a vision model isn't drowned in tokens, fit a thumbnail box, or normalize a batch to a common width. ffmpeg is the image tool — *do not hand-roll python/PIL* for this.

NOT for: cropping a region or letterboxing to an aspect ([image-crop-pad](image-crop-pad.md)); changing the file FORMAT ([image-convert](image-convert.md)); resizing a VIDEO stream ([transcode-to-h264](transcode-to-h264.md)). For just reading the current size: =ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=width,height -of csv=p=0 in.png=.

app :ffmpeg__resize_downscale_an_image_aspect_even_dims_quality do
  title       "ffmpeg — resize / downscale an image (aspect, even-dims, quality)"
  description "0.1.0 Use when you have a still image and need to scale it — downscale to a max dimension (e.g."
end

## The mental model: scale=W:H with computed sides

=scale=W:H= is the workhorse. The trick is what you put for W and H:

| Value | Meaning                                                    | |-------|-----------------------------------------------------------| | `800` | exact pixels                                              | | `-1`  | "compute to preserve aspect" (may produce an ODD number) | | `-2`  | same, but round to the nearest EVEN number               |

Use `-2` by default. Some encoders (h264/yuv420, a few image paths) reject odd dimensions; `-2` is the safe, aspect-preserving side. `-1` is fine for PNG/JPG output but bites you the moment the same recipe feeds a video.

## Workflow

### Downscale the long edge to a vision-friendly cap (no upscale)

Vision models read a ~1568px-max image fine; a bigger image just burns tokens. Cap the LONG edge at 1568 and keep aspect. The `min(1568,iw)` guard is what makes this *never upscale* — for a source already smaller than 1568, `min` picks the source width and nothing changes.

### verify input is a real image + read its dims
```bash
  test -f "$1" || { echo "input file $1 not found"; exit 1; }
  ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=width,height -of csv=p=0 "$1"
```

### cap WIDTH at 1568, keep aspect, never upscale (landscape source)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf "scale='min(1568,iw)':-2" out.png
```

### cap whichever edge is longer (portrait-safe)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf "scale='if(gt(iw,ih),min(1568,iw),-2)':'if(gt(iw,ih),-2,min(1568,ih))'" out.png
```

Why the long-edge form: a tall screenshot (e.g. 800×4000) is *portrait*, so capping width does nothing useful — you want the HEIGHT capped. The `gt(iw,ih)` branch caps whichever side is bigger.

### Fit inside a box without distortion

=force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease= scales down until the image fits ENTIRELY inside W×H, preserving aspect (one side will be smaller than the box). No stretching, no padding. Use this when you want "≤ this size, keep shape."

### fit inside 800×600, keep aspect (output may be e.g. 800×533)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf "scale=800:600:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease" out.png
```

`increase` instead would COVER the box (one side overflows) — pair it with a crop if you want exact box dims (see [[image-crop-pad]]). To pad the empty space to exact box dims, see the pad recipe there.

### Exact dimensions (will distort if aspect differs)

Only when you genuinely want exact pixels and accept the stretch:

### force exact 800×600 (can squash/stretch)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf scale=800:600 out.png
```

### Halve / double by ratio

`iw` / `ih` are the input dims; do math on them:

### half resolution
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf "scale=iw/2:ih/2" half.png
```

### confirm output dims
```bash
  test -f "$1" || { echo "output missing"; exit 1; }
  ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=width,height -of csv=p=0 "$1"
```

## Scaler quality — lanczos vs bilinear

ffmpeg's default swscale algorithm is `bicubic`. For DOWNSCALING, `lanczos` is sharper and the standard choice; `bilinear` is fastest but softest. Two equivalent ways to set it:

### lanczos inline in the filter (preferred — scoped to this scale)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -vf "scale=800:-2:flags=lanczos" out.png
```

### lanczos as a global swscale flag (applies to all scaling)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i in.png -sws_flags lanczos -vf "scale=800:-2" out.png
```

Guidance:
- *Downscaling photos/renders* → `lanczos` (crisp, minimal aliasing).
- *Pixel art / sharp UI screenshots you want crunchy* → `neighbor`
(nearest-neighbor; preserves hard edges, no blur).
- *Speed over quality, big batch* → `bilinear`.
- Upscaling: lanczos still helps but no algorithm invents detail.

## Upscaling caveats

Upscaling cannot add information — it interpolates, producing a soft or blocky result. ffmpeg has no AI super-resolution; for real upscaling reach for a dedicated model, not `scale`.

If you must enlarge for layout reasons, `lanczos` is the least-bad interpolator. *The bigger trap is upscaling by accident*: a plain =scale=1568:-2= ENLARGES any source narrower than 1568. Always use the `min(1568,iw)` guard (above) when the intent is "cap, don't grow."

## Avoiding re-encode artifacts

Every JPG re-save is lossy — resizing a JPG and writing JPG compounds artifacts. Mitigations:
- Resize FROM the highest-quality source you have, not a
previously-compressed copy.
- When writing JPG, set quality high (`-q:v 2`, lower = better).
- For an intermediate you'll process further, write PNG (lossless)
and only convert to JPG at the final step.
- PNG→resize→PNG is lossless on the encode side; only the
resampling itself changes pixels.

## Common pitfalls

1. *Odd dimensions rejected.* =scale=801:-1= can yield an odd height
     some encoders refuse. Use `-2` to force even. (Pure PNG/JPG usually tolerates odd, but `-2` costs nothing and is portable.)
2. *Accidental upscale.* =scale=1568:-2= grows a small image. Use
     `scale`'min(1568,iw)':-2= when the cap must never enlarge.
3. *Capping the wrong edge.* For portrait images, capping WIDTH does
     nothing — cap the longer edge with the `gt(iw,ih)` form.
4. *Default scaler is soft.* Bicubic is fine but `lanczos` is
     visibly sharper on downscales — set =flags=lanczos= for keepers.
5. *Quotes around scale expressions.* Expressions with `min`, `if`,
     `/` must be quoted (`scale`'min(1568,iw)':-2=) or the shell / ffmpeg parser mangles the commas and parens.
6. *force_original_aspect_ratio ≠ box dims.* With `decrease` the
     output is NOT W×H — it's the largest fit inside. If you need exact box dims, pad (see [[image-crop-pad]]).

## Verification checklist

- [ ] Output exists + `file` reports a real image type
- [ ] Long edge ≤ the cap when downscaling (`ffprobe` width/height)
- [ ] No accidental upscale (output ≤ source on the capped edge)
- [ ] Aspect preserved (W/H ratio matches source) unless you forced it
- [ ] For keepers: =flags=lanczos= used on the downscale

